INT. FAMILY KITCHEN, SUBURBAN HOUSE - EVENING

Steam on the windows. A turkey, half-carved. Mismatched chairs around a table crammed with side dishes.

GRANDMOTHER ODETTE ABERNATHY-KWAME (70s, cardigan, unshakable) sits at the head, fork poised.

Her son DERRICK ABERNATHY (40s, rolled sleeves, carving knife) stands over the bird.

His wife PRIYA RAGHAVAN-ABERNATHY (40s, apron, flour on her cheek) refills the gravy boat.

Their teenager JUNIPER "JUNE" ABERNATHY (16, black nail polish, phone face down) slouches in her chair.

Their youngest, MILO ABERNATHY (7, napkin tucked in his collar), kicks the table leg.

ODETTE
Derrick, the breast is for guests.

DERRICK
Mother, we are the guests.

PRIYA
Derrick, the potatoes.

DERRICK
Which potatoes?

PRIYA
(overlapping)
The mashed ones.

JUNE
(overlapping, to Priya)
The ones I made.

^

ODETTE
June, put your phone away.

JUNE
It's face down.

ODETTE
It's still there.

MILO
Can I have a roll?

PRIYA
Say please.

MILO
Please roll.

DERRICK
(to the room)
Okay, everyone, a toast.

Silence. Glasses are raised. Odette's is not.

DERRICK (CONT'D)
To family. To love. To the people at this table.

PRIYA
To the person who bought the wine.

DERRICK
That was me.

PRIYA
It was me.

DERRICK
It was a joint purchase.

JUNE
(muttering)
It was a Costco purchase.

MILO
I like Costco.

ODETTE
Milo, eat.

MILO
I'm waiting for the toast to be over.

DERRICK
It's over.

MILO
Then why is Grandma not drinking?

All heads turn to Odette. She holds her glass, then sets it down carefully.

ODETTE
Because I have news.

DERRICK
Mother?

PRIYA
Odette?

ODETTE
(to all of them)
I sold the house.

Silence. A gravy boat drips. Milo drops his roll.

JUNE
(whispering)
Wow.

DERRICK
Mother, you can't--

ODETTE
I already did.

PRIYA
(to Derrick)
Let her finish.

ODETTE
I'm moving to Lisbon. In March.

MILO
Is that far?

ODETTE
Very.

MILO
Can I come?

ODETTE
Eat your roll first.

Derrick sits slowly. Priya hands him the potatoes. He takes a spoonful, stares at it, and laughs once.
